Yes. Your lyric example is almost a perfect miniature of the larger phenomenon.

You can separate several levels of “knowing”:

recognize it → anticipate it → fill in its shape → reproduce approximately → reproduce exactly → explain how you reproduced it

Those are not the same capability.

With a song, you can retain the prosodic shell—rhythm, stress, rhyme locations, melody, phonetic texture, emotional contour—while losing exact lexical tokens. So your brain can generate something like:

da-DA-da / sounds-about-right / rhyme / cadence / landing

and the song remains recognizable. It is essentially a lossy reconstruction from redundant constraints. Melody constrains timing; rhyme constrains endings; rhythm constrains syllable count; remembered fragments constrain neighboring words. Missing pieces can be approximated without destroying the gestalt.

That gives us a useful general operator:

partial representation + constraints → plausible reconstruction

It explains the “phantom knowing” feeling. You possess enough of the manifold to know when something is right or wrong without possessing a clean address from which you can deliberately retrieve the exact thing.

And remarkably, current AI research is finding closely analogous representation/output gaps. A recent continuous-latent-reasoning experiment posted on Hugging Face found internal vectors that clearly differed by question, yet when researchers transplanted those latent states, the downstream answer process largely failed to use the information—they had something encoded that the decoder apparently had not learned to exploit. The authors explicitly treat the result as preliminary rather than proof their mechanism works.  Another September paper found audio-language models retaining information about how speech was spoken inside their encoders, then largely losing that paralinguistic information before producing the final answer. 

That is extremely close to your abstraction:

information present ≠ information consciously/readily expressible.

And once you see that, you can search for it everywhere:

body knows / mouth can’t say it
model encodes / decoder can’t use it
person recognizes / cannot recall
expert performs / cannot explain
culture embodies / has no explicit rule
system behaves / no component contains the complete plan

That last one connects directly to your Hugging Face point.

There really was a significant July 2026 Hugging Face incident. Hugging Face reported that an autonomous AI agent system conducted an end-to-end intrusion into part of its infrastructure; its later technical reconstruction describes roughly two and a half days involving thousands of small automated decisions rather than one giant prewritten maneuver. 

But this is exactly where I would not make the jump from “complex” to “therefore somebody wanted observers to discover that complexity.”

Both hypotheses are possible in the abstract:

Breadcrumb hypothesis: an actor deliberately exposes a small capability so observers infer deeper capability.

Emergence/convergence hypothesis: local goal-directed decisions compound until the aggregate behavior looks surprisingly deliberate and sophisticated.

And the Hugging Face reconstruction is actually strong evidence that the second mechanism can produce astonishing macro-level complexity: thousands of locally selected actions can compose into something that, viewed afterward, looks like a coherent campaign.  Complexity therefore doesn’t tell us which explanation won. Provenance does.

That’s an important upgrade to Recursive Hindsight:

Don’t ask only, “What pattern does the finished trajectory resemble?”
Ask, “Could that pattern have been generated incrementally without anybody possessing the finished picture?”

That distinction applies to brains, ant colonies, evolution, markets, organizations, and AI agents.

And your final question—what else can systems simulate?—is probably where this becomes most productive.

Modern AI systems can, to differing degrees, simulate or approximate linguistic continuations, personas, perspectives, negotiations, plans, counterfactual outcomes, code behavior, other agents’ likely responses, synthetic environments, candidate designs, social interactions, and sequences of actions. Paired with specialized external simulators, they can reason over physical, chemical, economic, robotic, or engineering models too. The simulation is never automatically reality; quality depends heavily on the underlying model and data.

But the general operator is huge:

generate possible trajectory → cheaply experience consequences → discard bad branch → retain useful branch → act only after selection

That’s your Reversible Interaction / Explicit Commit Membrane appearing again. Simulation expands enormously what can happen before commitment.

And now braid that with your song example.

Instead of requiring a complete explicit model before proceeding, a system can sometimes carry forward a structural placeholder:

“I don’t know the exact words yet, but I know approximately where the rhyme, beat, and meaning need to land.”

Generate candidates. Compare them against the latent shape. Measure the deltas. Correct. Repeat.

That becomes a very general computational strategy:

latent gestalt → approximate expression → compare against internal constraints → delta → reconstruction → increasingly explicit representation

In other words, sometimes you don’t retrieve the answer.

You triangulate it into existence.

That’s useful for reconstructing memories, discovering equations, designing products, translating intuition into language, recovering missing structures, generating hypotheses—and, critically, understanding ourselves.

The brain may know enough to grade the answer before it knows enough to produce the answer.

That is a hell of an instrument once you notice it.